<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<topic id="252dfa96-85bf-4da0-917e-6f837a2b746d" revisionNumber="1">
  <developerConceptualDocument
    xmlns="http://ddue.schemas.microsoft.com/authoring/2003/5"
    xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">

    <introduction>
		<para>With DemiCode Scheduler one usually want to perform a task when a schedule fires.
                If <link xlink:href="f592f4a7-ecd8-4de0-949c-8c45dcf87b59">the built-in tasks</link> does not cover
                your needs you can easily implement your own task. This section describes the necessary
                steps to create and start using your own task.</para>
    </introduction>

   
                                    
    
 
<section>
  <title>Implementing the ITask interface</title>
  <content>
    <para>Every task must implement the
                <codeEntityReference qualifyHint="false">T:DemiCode.Scheduling.Tasks.ITask</codeEntityReference>
                interface. This interface is simple and requires only the
                <codeEntityReference qualifyHint="false" autoUpgrade="true">M:DemiCode.Scheduling.Tasks.ITask.Execute(DemiCode.Logging.ILoggerFactory)</codeEntityReference>
                method.</para>
		<code language="c#">
public class MyTask : ITask
{
    public void Execute(IDictionary parameters, ILoggerFactory loggerFactory)
    {
        // Do some work... 
    }
}
		</code>
                <para>
                    The class can be placed in either a standalone .NET library or in the application
                    that will host the scheduler. We decide to place the class in a library called TaskLibrary.
                </para>
  </content>
</section>
      
            
            

<section>
  <title>Hooking the task to a schedule</title>
  <content>
    <para>You assign tasks to schedules through configuration. Here is a sample configuration
                that configures a scheduler that executes our task every five seconds.
                <code language="xml">
&lt;scheduler&gt;
    &lt;tasks&gt;
        &lt;task type=&quot;MyTask, TaskLibrary&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;/tasks&gt;
    &lt;IntervalSchedule&gt;
          &lt;start&gt;2007-11-01 12:00:00&lt;/start&gt;
          &lt;interval&gt;00:00:05&lt;/interval&gt;
    &lt;/IntervalSchedule&gt;
&lt;/scheduler&gt;
				</code>
	</para>
  </content>
</section>
                
            
            
 
<section>
  <title>Passing parameters to your task</title>
  <content>
    <para>
    			<para>
                    Parameters can be passed to the task by adding name/value attributes in the configuration
                    XML. The parameter name can be any valid attribute name.
                </para>
                <para>
                    The following sample shows how to configure the parameter and then some code to
                    retreive it.
                </para>
                <code langugae="xml">
&lt;scheduler&gt;
    &lt;tasks&gt;
        &lt;task type=&quot;MyTask, TaskLibrary&quot; server=&quot;machine-001&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;/tasks&gt;
    &lt;IntervalSchedule&gt;
          &lt;start&gt;2007-11-01 12:00:00&lt;/start&gt;
          &lt;interval&gt;00:00:05&lt;/interval&gt;
    &lt;/IntervalSchedule&gt;
&lt;/scheduler&gt;
				</code>
                <code language="c#">
public class MyTask : ITask
{
    public void Execute(IDictionary parameters, ILoggerFactory loggerFactory)
    {
        string server = (string)parameters[&quot;server&quot;];
        
        // Do some work...
    }
}
            	</code>
	</para>
  </content>
</section>
               
            
            
 
<section>
  <title>Logging from your task</title>
  <content>
    <para>
    			<para>
                    Often it is usefull to log status or error messages during task execution. To integrate
                    logging with the same logging framework used by the Scheduler you should use the
                    provided
                    <codeEntityReference qualifyHint="false">T:DemiCode.Logging.ILoggerFactory</codeEntityReference>
                    object. The factory creates a logger provided a logger name or a type. The type
                    overload uses the type name as logger name.
				</para>
                <para>
                    <legacyBold>Note:</legacyBold>&#160;DemiCode Scheduler will automatically log the task start,
                    task end and any unhandled exception thrown during task execution.</para>
                <para>
                    Here is a sample that logs the provided parameter value:</para>
                <code language="c#">
public class MyTask : ITask
{
    public void Execute(IDictionary parameters, ILoggerFactory loggerFactory)
    {
        string server = (string)parameters[&quot;server&quot;];
        
        ILogger logger = loggerFactory.Create(typeof(MyTask));
        logger.Info(&quot;Executing using server name {0}&quot;, server);
        
        // Do some work...
    }
}
            </code>
    </para>
  </content>
</section>
               
            
            
 
<section>
  <title>Whats next?</title>
  <content>
    <para>Use the <link xlink:href="f0361e4e-a359-4f54-a200-6e8a11b94dff">SchedulerService</link> sample to
                try out your new task or write your own host.</para>
  </content>
</section>
               
            
    

